WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
Speaker 1: Yesterday, we had a nearly&nbsp;
14 hour livestream of the second day of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:10.800
Joe Biden's Supreme Court nominee,&nbsp;
Judge Kaitano G. Brown Jackson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:18.480
confirmation hearing, during which she was asked&nbsp;
about 30 minutes of questions by each of the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.960
group, is twenty two senators on the Judiciary&nbsp;
Committee. I believe they got to 20 and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.320
the last two were going to go this morning and&nbsp;
we're going to get to some clips of what was asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.000
and we're going to talk about all of that. But&nbsp;
before doing any of that, I think it's important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:40.000
to take an objective look at how qualified&nbsp;
Judge Jackson is and by any any, any measure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:50.080
any objective measure. Judge Katju Brown Jackson&nbsp;
is, or would be one of the most qualified Supreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:58.000
Court justices in recent history. And this is&nbsp;
not going by vague, ideology based opinions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:04.480
or anything subjective merely looking at the&nbsp;
facts of her experience. It is hard to find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:09.440
more qualified Supreme Court justice recently&nbsp;
now. I know many of you, as we get into this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:14.880
are going to say, Wait, hold on a second,&nbsp;
David. If Amy Coney Barrett can be on the court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.960
if Kavanaugh can be on the court, why is this&nbsp;
even a conversation? And of course, you would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:27.280
completely correct to frame it in those terms. But&nbsp;
because it is worth actually thinking about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:32.320
experience of a potential Supreme Court justice.&nbsp;
We're going to look at it and there's a very good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:40.560
chart out which looks at the risks the current&nbsp;
justices and compares Judge Jackson's experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:48.800
And what you find when you look at it is that&nbsp;
she checks more boxes than do any of the justices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:56.400
currently on the court. When you look at whether&nbsp;
she went to an Ivy League law school, which I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:01.840
don't think is the the standard by which we should&nbsp;
evaluate qualifications, but one of the things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:07.520
that many say is, well, where where was the legal&nbsp;
education? All of the justices currently on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.560
court other than Barrett, went to an Ivy League&nbsp;
law school, and so did Judge Jackson. When you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:19.920
look at which justices were clerks on the Supreme&nbsp;
Court, Clarence Thomas, Alito and Sotomayor were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:27.280
not. The other justices on the court were. And&nbsp;
Judge Jackson was a Supreme Court clerk, great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.960
in terms of who was a public defender. None of&nbsp;
them. None of them. Judge Jackson actually brings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:37.840
unique experience or would bring unique experience&nbsp;
to the court by virtue of having been a public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:45.600
defender. None of the nine current justices did&nbsp;
that as a job. Only Justice Breyer, who would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:53.360
replaced by Judge Jackson, was on a sentencing&nbsp;
commission and indeed Judge Clayton G. Brown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.800
Jackson was on a sentencing commission. Only&nbsp;
Judge Sotomayor of the justices currently on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:05.120
the court was a district judge. Judge Jackson&nbsp;
has that experience in her background as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:14.000
and currently all justices other than Elena Kagan&nbsp;
were judges on courts of appeals. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:24.320
Judge Jackson was a judge on the Court of Appeals.&nbsp;
Now I so so the objective case is as or more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:30.080
qualified than any of the justices currently on&nbsp;
the court. Now, I think there's one interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:36.480
thing to talk about here. You and I might look&nbsp;
at the experience of Judge Jackson and say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.400
Wow, that's that's amazing. And it's actually&nbsp;
fascinating that public defender experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:48.640
and sentencing commission experience and district&nbsp;
judge experience, that's unique. That's a unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.800
level of experience. Nobody on the court right&nbsp;
now has had experience in those three areas. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.040
there's a liability that comes with that, which&nbsp;
we learned about yesterday during the hearing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:06.000
And it is that it provides a significant amount&nbsp;
of fodder for which she can be attacked. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:13.120
why did you do this specific sentence in this&nbsp;
case or why did you rule in this particular way?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.960
Why did you defend this particular client, even&nbsp;
though lawyers defend clients? That's what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:25.840
do. And the analogy here would be Imagine that&nbsp;
I ran for something you and I might say, Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:32.080
you know, what's? What's great is I have over 10&nbsp;
years of talking about so many different issues.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.360
You can just kind of look up and see what my&nbsp;
thoughts are. But the truth is that if you did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.480
let's call it a political colonoscopy or, you&nbsp;
know, a political body cavity search and you went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:48.560
through everything I've ever said on the show, you&nbsp;
know, hundreds and hundreds and hundreds of hours,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.480
you would without a doubt be able to find&nbsp;
things that don't represent number one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.720
What I would call the best of my show, that might&nbsp;
be opinions which have changed because. Is either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.280
my opinions have changed or there's different&nbsp;
information that has now come out, which has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.360
led me to have to revise opinions. So without&nbsp;
a doubt, if I were to run for something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.640
although I see it as a positive thing that I have&nbsp;
this long track record of people being able to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:18.640
the evolution of my views. You would be able to&nbsp;
troll that and find things to take out of context&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:24.240
or isolate that I without a doubt would make&nbsp;
me look bad. I'm I. It would make me look bad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:28.800
And along the same way, although you and I&nbsp;
might say wow, public defender experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.040
and sentencing commission experience and&nbsp;
district judge experience, that's unbelievable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:39.280
What we saw yesterday during the first day of&nbsp;
questions asked to Judge Jackson and what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.640
are already seeing today during the second day of&nbsp;
questions, which is underway right now is we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:53.920
as we are recording today's show, is that it also&nbsp;
can serve as. A sort of catalyst or as a basis to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:06:00.800
focus an extraordinarily narrowly on particular&nbsp;
decisions and take them out of context and and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:10.240
whatever else. One other thing. There was a lot of&nbsp;
concern yesterday from Republican senators about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.640
sentences given by Judge Jackson on what? Now&nbsp;
I want to be clear some people call it child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:24.560
porn. I don't think that's the right word because&nbsp;
pornography is almost always legal and involving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.880
consenting adults and is a business to some&nbsp;
degree, to just put child on that and to pretend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:37.280
what we're talking about is child exploitation,&nbsp;
right? I mean, we're not talking about filmmakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.280 --> 00:06:44.000
when we talk about people producing or whatever&nbsp;
these images, but for what they call child porn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:49.680
which I don't think is the right term for it. They&nbsp;
talked a lot about her departures from the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.760
penalties as sort of a liability. But the truth&nbsp;
is the people doing that like, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:07:01.360
Republican Senator Josh Hawley. He has previously&nbsp;
supported many judicial candidates that departed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.720
from the maximum, sentencing numerous times.&nbsp;
So they focused in on that. But significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.520
hypocrisy there. So that's the qualifications.&nbsp;
Let's now go to some of the questioning.

